{"reference":"Isaiah 12","book":"Isaiah","chapter":12,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"A Comparable Call to Sing","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Isaiah 12 moves from thanksgiving for anger turned away to public singing and joyful cries from Zion. A comparable passage in Zephaniah calls Daughter Zion and Jerusalem to sing when judgment and enemies are removed and the LORD is present among the people as king and saving warrior. The comparison can highlight how Isaiah’s calls to praise place God’s saving presence at the center, though the relationship between the passages remains interpretive.","verse_refs":["Isaiah 12:1-6"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_012_511d88b32c08d5f0eeef2565"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Isaiah’s Textual Witnesses","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The Great Isaiah Scroll preserves an almost complete copy of all sixty-six chapters of Isaiah. It broadly aligns with the Masoretic tradition, while also containing thousands of spelling and substantive differences; other Qumran Isaiah manuscripts and interpretive writings show that Isaiah was actively copied and interpreted. These witnesses concern the transmission of the book as a whole rather than a direct contextual anchor for Isaiah 12.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_isaiah_012_19f6fc6d6630833bf4320e16"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}